Yes. And I think you’re describing a remarkably powerful combination: the ability to recognize value, the charisma to communicate it, and the generosity to give that recognition away.

Because we’re not just mirrors reflecting what other people already are.

We can become mirrors that help people discover what they haven’t yet recognized about themselves.

And sometimes being seen differently changes what someone believes they’re capable of becoming.

Appraisal is more than judgment.

Think about appraisal as a skill in a role-playing game. You encounter an unfamiliar object, creature, or person and recognize properties, possibilities, or value that others might overlook.

But in life, appraisal has multiple registers.

* Recognition: Seeing qualities other people miss.
* Valuation: Understanding what those qualities are worth in a particular context.
* Projection: Imagining what those qualities might become under different circumstances.
* Communication: Helping someone understand what you’ve recognized.
* Empowerment: Giving them the opportunity to develop or use those qualities themselves.

Now introduce charisma.

Someone can recognize your potential and never tell you.

Someone else can tell you but communicate it so poorly that you don’t believe them.

And someone with genuine insight and charisma can articulate something about you so precisely that you suddenly recognize it yourself.

The gift isn’t necessarily the compliment. It’s the new perspective.

That’s where the mirror becomes transformative.

But mirrors can distort, too.

Every appraisal contains some of the observer.

Someone might see weakness where another sees restraint. Arrogance where another sees confidence. Recklessness where another sees courage.

And if the observer carries enough authority or charisma, their interpretation can begin influencing the identity of the person they’re observing.

That’s a tremendous responsibility.

A teacher who recognizes a student’s talent might help cultivate it. A parent who repeatedly labels a child incapable might unintentionally constrain their confidence.

Neither appraisal is automatically a complete or accurate account of that person.

Charisma amplifies appraisal. It doesn’t guarantee its accuracy.

And this brings us back to our discussion about trust and The Prestige: recognition feels especially meaningful when it comes from someone whose judgment we respect.

Now here’s the part about gifting it.

You can give somebody money, opportunities, knowledge, or possessions.

But you can also give them a way of seeing.

You can recognize someone’s potential, explain why you see it, teach them how to recognize it themselves, and eventually enable them to recognize it in other people.

That’s a gift that can propagate.

One person feels genuinely recognized. They become more confident in an ability. They develop it. They recognize something valuable in someone else.

A chain of mirrors, each revealing something the previous one couldn’t.

And there’s a beautiful distinction:

You don’t have to create someone’s worth to help them recognize it.

Nor do they have to accept your appraisal. A good mirror offers a perspective without demanding that the person become its reflection.

I think this ties together several of our threads: generational knowledge, semantic point guards, the power of reframing, reciprocity, and learning through other people.

Maybe that’s one of the highest uses of appraisal:

See the treasure. Recognize its possibilities. Help its owner see it. And don’t mistake discovering its value for owning it.

Sometimes the most valuable thing you can give someone is an accurate glimpse of something wonderful they already possess.

And sometimes, if you’re fortunate, somebody returns the favor. ;)